WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:04.059
In this video, we'll look at some additional tips and

2
00:00:04.059 --> 00:00:06.240
tricks for OSC as well as point to

3
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.300
some resources you can utilize when

4
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.880
implementing OSC for your own projects.

5
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.619
Some tips and tricks we used before

6
00:00:13.619 --> 00:00:17.019
is actually to convert everything to strings.

7
00:00:17.019 --> 00:00:18.920
As we know in Unreal Engine,

8
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.780
almost every types of

9
00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.300
variable is able to be converted into a string.

10
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.399
So for example, in the screenshot we showed here,

11
00:00:27.559 --> 00:00:31.960
we are sending two different OSC messages,

12
00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.719
all using just three string values.

13
00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:37.759
So on the left side, we see we're sending things like

14
00:00:37.759 --> 00:00:39.840
Dickens and a full value and

15
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.600
the Boolean value all converted into strings.

16
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.640
On the right-hand side, we're again sending one string value,

17
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.159
one integer value converted to string,

18
00:00:50.159 --> 00:00:53.119
and one more enum converted to string.

19
00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:55.399
So that's just an easy way for us to

20
00:00:55.399 --> 00:00:58.960
quickly utilize the same event over and over,

21
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.399
but sending various types of values.

22
00:01:02.399 --> 00:01:04.790
Do note strings are

23
00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:07.549
a more expensive data type than something like a Boolean,

24
00:01:07.549 --> 00:01:09.290
which I believe is only one byte.

25
00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:11.030
Strings can be much, much more.

26
00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:14.069
So if you do run into issues with bottlenecking for data,

27
00:01:14.069 --> 00:01:16.510
then perhaps strings are not the best choice.

28
00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.969
In these examples, we're utilizing functions which

29
00:01:19.969 --> 00:01:23.049
take in strings and other data that are necessary,

30
00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:26.569
and then within the function that data is sent over OSC.

31
00:01:26.569 --> 00:01:29.250
As previously mentioned, it's important to use

32
00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:32.090
unique names for both your server and your clients.

33
00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:35.530
Anything with the same name will actually override each other,

34
00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.489
not unlike when you have input set to be consumed,

35
00:01:39.489 --> 00:01:42.109
in which case here if two things have the same name,

36
00:01:42.109 --> 00:01:44.609
then only the second one that is

37
00:01:44.609 --> 00:01:48.450
created would work and the original earlier one would not.

38
00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:49.890
So just again, reminder,

39
00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.079
give everything a unique name.

40
00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:55.840
Another reminder to a similar issue.

41
00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.439
For OSC server, we need to make sure

42
00:01:58.439 --> 00:02:02.480
each port only has one OSC server listening to it.

43
00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:05.920
If we have two OSC server listening to

44
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.120
the same port and live in say in a different actor,

45
00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.319
but they are both in the same level,

46
00:02:11.319 --> 00:02:13.900
it might encounter issues that

47
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.319
only one server is able to listen to it,

48
00:02:16.319 --> 00:02:19.599
and the other is not able to receive anything.

49
00:02:19.599 --> 00:02:24.840
Make sure that each port only has one listen server

50
00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:30.280
that's listening to the messages.

51
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.330
Lastly, certainly don't feel the need to type these indirectly.

